Let $x$ be a real nonzero number. If we plug $x$ in for $x,$ we get
\[f(x) + 2f \left ( \frac{1}{x} \right ) = 3x.\]If we plug $1/x$ in for $x,$ we get
\[f \left ( \frac{1}{x} \right ) + 2f(x) = \frac{3}{x}.\]If we solve the system of equations above by multiplication, we get
\begin{align*}
f(x) + 2f \left ( \frac{1}{x} \right ) &= 3x \\
3x &= 2f \left ( \frac{1}{x} \right ) + 2f \left ( \frac{1}{x} \right ) \\
5x &= 2f \left ( \frac{1}{x} \right ) + 2f(x).
\end{align*}Dividing by 2 and multiplying the minus sign by 3, we get
\[3x = f(x) + f(-x).\]Because $x = (-x),$ it must follow that $f(x) = f(-x),$ and thus our answer is $\boxed{0}.$[asy]
